<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-6-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Working with Radicals</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>In this Lesson we use the product and quotient rules for radicals to simplify radical expressions, including rationalizing the denominator.</p>
<p>This is a skills lesson, and you might want to decide what level of facility your students need with radicals. Begin by explaining the distinction between evaluating a radical (e.g. <m>\sqrt{45} \approx 6.708</m>) and simplifying a radical (<m>\sqrt{45} = 3\sqrt{5}</m>). It is probably most effective to work through these Activities with the class, completing the first example in each group for them, and then asking groups to try the other examples. Walk around and help individuals when they get stuck.</p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
  <li><p>Simplifying radicals</p></li>    
  <li><p>Adding or subtracting like radicals</p></li>  
  <li><p>Simplifying products or quotients of radicals</p></li>   
  <li><p>Rationalizing the denominator</p></li>
</ul></p>
</assemblage>

<activity><title>Simplifying Radicals</title>

<introduction><p>We use the product and quotient rules for radicals to simplify radicals. To "simplify" a radical does not mean to find a decimal approximation using a calculator! The simplified expression is an exact equivalent for the original radical, not an approximation. For example,
    <md><mrow>\sqrt{12} = \sqrt{4 \cdot 3} = \sqrt{4}~ \sqrt{3} = 2\sqrt{3}</mrow>
        <mrow>\sqrt[3]{40} = \sqrt[3]{8 \cdot 5} = \sqrt[3]{8}~ \sqrt[3]{5} = 2\sqrt[3]{5}</mrow></md>
You can use your calculator to verify that the simplified expression has the same decimal approximation as the original radical.</p>
</introduction>

<p><ol>
  <li><p>Simplify the radicals. Look for a perfect square or a perfect cube that divides evenly into the radicand.</p>
  <p><ol cols="2">
    <li><p><m>\sqrt{45}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt{200}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt{48}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{81}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{72}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{250}</m></p></li>
  </ol></p></li>

    <li><p>Fill in the blanks.</p>
    <p><ol>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{x^6} = </m> <fillin/> because (<fillin/>)<m>^2 = x^6</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{x^{15}} = </m> <fillin/> because (<fillin/>)<m>^3 = x^{15}</m></p></li> 
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[4]{x^{12}} = </m> <fillin/> because (<fillin/>)<m>^4 = x^{12}</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>

  <li><p>Simplify the radicals.</p>
  <p><ol cols="2">
    <li><p><m>\sqrt{81x^8}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt{16x^{16}}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{8x^9}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt[4]{81x^{20}}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt{\dfrac{9a^8}{16b^4}}</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{\dfrac{-27y^9}{125z^6}}</m></p></li>
  </ol></p></li>

    <li><p>To simplify the square root of an odd power of <m>x</m>, we factor off <m>x</m>. For example,
        <me>\sqrt{x^{13}} = \sqrt{x^{12} \cdot x} = \sqrt{x^{12}} \cdot \sqrt{x} = x^6 \sqrt{x}</me>
  For cube roots, we factor out a power whose exponent is a multiple of 3.
        <me>\sqrt[3]{x^8} = \sqrt[3]{x^6 \cdot x^2} = \sqrt[3]{x^6} \cdot \sqrt[3]{x^2} = x^2 \sqrt[3]{x^2}</me></p>

          <aside component="instructor"><p>1(4) Show the class how to factor the radicand into "perfect squares" and "left-overs":
              <me> \sqrt{72b^{16}c^{15}} = \sqrt{36 b^{16}c^{14} \cdot 2c}</me></p></aside>

    <p><ol cols="2">
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{12t^5}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{27w^9}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{80a^5 b^4}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{72b^{16}c^{15}}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{32 m^{11}}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{64 w^4 z^2}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{81 a^{12} b^8}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{40x^{14} y^9}</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>
  </ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Operations on Radicals</title>

<introduction><p>We can combine the radicands in products or quotients of radicals as long as both radicals have the same index, but we cannot combine radicands in sums or differences. You can check that:
    <md><mrow> \sqrt{12}~\sqrt{3} = \sqrt{36}~~~~~~~~\amp \text{and}~~~~~~~~ \dfrac{\sqrt{12}}{\sqrt{3}} = \sqrt{4}</mrow>
        <mrow> \text{but}~~\sqrt{12}+\sqrt{3} \not= \sqrt{15}~~~~~~~~\amp \text{and}~~~~~~~~ \sqrt{12}-\sqrt{3} \not= \sqrt{9}</mrow></md>
  </p></introduction>

         <aside component="instructor"><p>Help students recognize the difference between a product of radicals and a sum of radicals, while emphasizing that the rules for simplifying them are quite different.</p></aside>   

<p><ol>
    <li><p>Simplify the following products and quotients.</p>
    <p><ol cols="2">
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{4x^2}~\sqrt[3]{16x}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[4]{2m^3}~\sqrt[4]{8m}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{18xy^3}}{\sqrt{2y}}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{\sqrt[3]{200a^8}}{\sqrt[3]{5a^2}}</m></p></li>
  </ol></p></li>

    <li><p>True or false?</p>
    <p><ol cols="2">
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{7} + \sqrt{7} = \sqrt{14}~</m>?</p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{7} + \sqrt{7} = 2\sqrt{7}~</m>?</p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{7} - \sqrt{5} = \sqrt{2}~</m>?</p></li>
      <li><p><m>6\sqrt{5} - 4\sqrt{5} = 2\sqrt{5}~</m>?</p></li>
    </ol></p></li>

  <li><p>Simplify and combine like terms.</p>
    <p><ol cols="2">
      <li><p><m>5\sqrt{6} - 2\sqrt{6} + 6\sqrt{2}~</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>8\sqrt[3]{a^2} - 5\sqrt[3]{a} - 5\sqrt[3]{a^2}~</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>2\sqrt{75} + 3\sqrt{48} </m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt[3]{81} + \sqrt[3]{24} - 3\sqrt[3]{3}~</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>

  <li><p>Simplify the products. Use the distributive law when appropriate.</p>
      <p><ol cols="2">
      <li><p><m>(3\sqrt{2})(2\sqrt{5})</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>(3\sqrt{5})(2\sqrt{5})</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>(2\sqrt{15})(3\sqrt{6}) </m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>5(2\sqrt{3} - \sqrt{5})</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>4\sqrt{2}(4\sqrt{3} + 2\sqrt{6})</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>(3\sqrt{x} - \sqrt{5})(2\sqrt{x} + 3\sqrt{5})</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>
  </ol></p>
  </activity>

<activity><title>Rationalizing the Denominator</title>

<p><ol>
  <li><p>Simplify by rationalizing the denominator.</p>
  <p><ol cols="2">
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{10}{\sqrt{5}} </m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{6}{\sqrt{12}}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{\dfrac{27x}{20}}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{6\sqrt{2}}{3\sqrt{v}}</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>

  <li><p>Multiply each binomial by its conjugate.</p>
  <p><ol>
      <li><p><m>(3 + \sqrt{7})(3 - \sqrt{7})</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>(\sqrt{6} - \sqrt{3})(\sqrt{6} + \sqrt{3})</m></p></li>
    </ol></p>
  <p>Do you see a short-cut for the product?</p>
  <p> Hint: What is the product <m>(a+b)(a-b)~</m>?</p>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>3(3) Rationalizing denominators somehow reinforces students' misconception that <m>(a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2</m>, so take the opportunity to compare <m>(a+b)(a-b)</m> with <m>(a+b)^2</m>.</p></aside></li>

  <li><p>Use a conjugate to rationalize the denominator.</p>
  <p><ol cols="2">
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{3} - \sqrt{2}} </m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{y}{\sqrt{5}-y}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{6} - 3}{2 - \sqrt{6}}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{x} + \sqrt{y}}{\sqrt{x} - \sqrt{y}}</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>
  </ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
  <li><p>Explain the difference between simplifying a radical and evaluating a radical.</p></li>
  <li><p>Explain how to simplify a cube root.  List the cubes of the first six whole numbers.</p></li>
  <li><p>Compare the rules for adding radicals and multiplying radicals.  Illustrate by computing <m>~4\sqrt[3]{2x^2} + 3\sqrt[3]{2x^2}~</m>    and <m>~4\sqrt[3]{2x^2} \cdot 3\sqrt[3]{2x^2}~</m>.</p></li>
  <li><p>Explain why we use a conjugate to rationalize a binomial denominator.</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
  <li><p>Explain the difference between simplifying a radical and evaluating a radical.</p></li>
  <li><p>Explain how to simplify a cube root.  List the cubes of the first six whole numbers.</p></li>
  <li><p>Compare the rules for adding radicals and multiplying radicals. Illustrate by computing <m>4\sqrt[3]{2x^2} + 3\sqrt[3]{2x^2}</m>  and  <m>4\sqrt[3]{2x^2} \cdot 3\sqrt[3]{2x^2}</m>.</p></li>
  <li><p>Explain why we use a conjugate to rationalize a binomial denominator.</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Which of the following is true?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>\sqrt{4+x^2} = 2 + x</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt[3]{a^2b^4} = \sqrt[3]{a^2}\sqrt[3]{b^4}</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt{\dfrac{1}{m^9}}=\dfrac{1}{m^3}</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt[3]{x^3-8y^3} = x - 2y</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><m>\sqrt{x} \sqrt[3]{x} = </m>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>\sqrt[6]{x^5}</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt[6]{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt[5]{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt[3]{x^2}</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>To simplify <m>\sqrt[3]{N}</m>, we</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>Use a calculator to compute <m>N^{1/3}</m></li>   
        <li>Take the square root of <m>N</m> twice</li>
        <li>Look for perfect cubes that are factors of <m>N</m></li> 
        <li>Subtract the largest perfect cube from <m>N</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Which equation is false?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>\sqrt{2x}+\sqrt{2x^3}=\sqrt{2x}(1+x)</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt{2x}\sqrt{2x^3}=2x^2</m></li>
        <li><m>\dfrac{2x}{\sqrt{2x^3}} = \dfrac{\sqrt{2x}}{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>\sqrt{4x^2}-\sqrt{2x^2} = 2x</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>